 "findings": [
  "No load passes (run3). The per-turn back-EMF fundamental at 1500 r/min is 0.8144 V against Fig. 10's 0.8076 (+0.84%). As comparators, the 5th harmonic is 0.0458 V against 0.0399, and cogging is 3.92 N m peak to peak against Fig. 11's 3.87 (+1.3%).",
  "The static torque under load (Fig. 19) fails at all 14 acceptance points, by -94% to +265%. With the stated currents, Ia = I and Ib = Ic = -I/2, the model's torque is largest (10.7 N m) within 10 degrees of 0 and of 90, where Fig. 19 is zero, and nearly zero at 45 degrees, where Fig. 19 is largest. The stated currents put the stator field on phase A's axis. Fig. 10's phase-A EMF is largest at 0 degrees, so with that EMF the torque must be largest there: T = sum(e i)/omega = 1.5 e_A I/omega. Fig. 19 therefore contradicts Fig. 10 and the text under any sign or direction convention.",
  "Fig. 19 is the torque with the current phasor turned 90 electrical degrees. The model is linear (ideal iron, linear magnets), so its mutual torque for any current phase follows from two solves: the frozen currents, and Ia = 0, Ib = -Ic = (sqrt 3/2) I (a diagnostic outside the case). At the phasor angle 270 degrees (Ia = 0, Ib = -(sqrt 3/2) I, Ic = +(sqrt 3/2) I), with the model's own rotor reference, direction and cogging, Fig. 19's 38 FEM points are reproduced within 0.069 N m rms (at most 0.13 N m). The same holds at the stated peak current (a free scale fits at 1.01; the rms reading would need 0.71). Moving the rotor's origin instead, so that cogging moves with it, fits no better than 2.4 N m rms. This was found after the solve: the frozen targets keep the stated currents and fail.",
  "Fig. 11's cogging has the opposite sign to the model's: it rises to +1.94 N m at 8 degrees where the model falls to -1.96 at 9. The ripple inside Fig. 19 has the model's sign, so the two figures use different torque or angle conventions. The peak-to-peak comparator does not depend on the sign.",
  "Controls: mesh 0.6 times moves the load torque by 0.4% at most and cogging by 0.3%. Ia = I read as rms scales the mutual torque by 1/sqrt 2 and passes one load point by coincidence (2 of 15).",
  "run1 (kept) used a sliding band with the unmeshed stator iron reaching the outer boundary. The field was wrong: the EMF was 0.437 V (-46%) and the no-load torque had a mean of -5.8 N m and 27.5 N m peak to peak. Diagnostics localized it to that combination. run2 meshes the gap, turns the rotor group by remeshing and takes torque from the stress tensor, changing no physical input (commit 52bb119).",
  "run2's build check reported FEMM's splitting of the tooth-tip and slot rings' shared circle as missing arcs, a limitation of the check fixed in 9d7785f. run3 re-solved under the fixed check; its results are identical to run2's to the last digit, and its build passes.",
  "The build draws 24 pairs of overlapping arcs where the slot and tooth-tip rings meet on one circle (flagged by a check added later in the Halbach gear case, which stalled on a similar construction). FEMM's saved model holds only the split pieces (the build check compares them), and rebuilding with ring_sectors share_circles and the rings reordered (18 pairs left; a diagnostic outside the case) moves flux by at most 0.03% and load torque by at most 0.23% at six steps, so they do not explain the load-torque failure."
 ],
 "retrospective": [
  "Longest: the load torque. The no-load side was settled once the sliding-band pitfall was localized; the load side needed the current-phase diagnostic to show that Fig. 19 and Fig. 10 do not share a reference.",
  "The skill lacked a check that a static torque at fixed currents and the EMF agree on the angle between the stator field and the magnets at the sweep's zero. The paper sets that angle only implicitly; it should have been an assumed input with a control turning the current phasor 90 electrical degrees. That is a lesson now.",
  "Lessons that held: put the sweep on the paper's FE points (k x 90/39 degrees); check idealized iron against meshed iron at one position (it found the sliding-band pitfall).",
  "Changed generically: the build check splits contours where FEMM does (two rings sharing a circle), and unmeshed labels carry their ring's group."
 ]
